From 34715f6e3433d376b34f19c4191b9c6e8efefb95 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Code Review <review@local>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 11:38:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] TUI screens + ip:port peering + rebuilt web UI
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three things, plus the root cause of "the web UI is broken".

--- why the web UI was broken ---
Not styling. Two dead endpoints. The template called POST /api/send and
POST /api/scan; the real routes are POST /api/message/send and GET
/api/peers/scan. It also posted {chat_id, text} where the backend reads
{peer_id, plaintext}. So sending, receiving and peer loading all failed
silently while settings and theme — which touch different routes — kept
working. That's exactly the reported symptom pattern, and it's why a
restyle would have fixed nothing.

Found it by diffing every fetch() URL in the template against
web.py's actual url_map rather than reading the code. That check is
cheap and would have caught this at any point.

--- rebuilt web UI ---
templates/chat.html rewritten. Same palette and fonts, unchanged. Every
endpoint verified against url_map before writing a line. Simpler
structure: one sidebar with chats/peers tabs, one chat panel, one
settings modal (identity / profiles / network). Keeps what was already
verified working — CSRF header on mutating requests, WebSocket push,
mobile slide-over layout, theme toggle.

Two real bugs found while testing the rebuild:
- switching sidebar tabs rendered from the cached array without
  refetching, so "peers" looked empty even when peers were discovered.
- the polling fallback was gated on !document.hidden, which froze the
  entire UI in a background tab.

Verified end to end in a real browser against two live nodes: unlock,
discovery, open chat, send — and confirmed the OTHER node actually
received it, not just that it rendered locally.

--- TUI ---
:profiles, :new-profile <name>, :settings, :help and ? for help. New
screens are full overlays that own their key input, so chat navigation
underneath is untouched. :settings edits display name, LAN mode and all
three DHT settings; booleans toggle on Enter, text opens an input box.
:new-profile prompts for a passphrase (twice), creates the identity via
the same shared backend call the web UI uses, and starts the profile's
web UI.

Also fixed the reported display corruption: "[transport] send failed
..." was the console log handler writing to a terminal curses owns.
Muted the console handler only — file logs still work.

--- ip:port ---
Previously rejected outright, honestly, because no handshake existed —
a chat_id must be a real user_id or nothing can be encrypted. Added
one: GET /identity on the transport server, consumed by
Node.connect_to_address(). Same key-pinning rule as LAN discovery: an
address claiming a contact you already have but with different keys is
refused. Typed by hand is not more trustworthy than broadcast.

Tested with network_bind=host on both nodes so discovery was fully
disabled — it connected and delivered a message anyway, which proves
it works independently of discovery rather than accidentally through it.

--- PID ---
Creating a profile now prints its pid to the terminal that launched the
parent, from both the TUI and the web UI, and returns it in the API
response. A browser status line is gone on the next page load and you
need that pid to stop the thing.

--- test updates ---
Two of my own tests asserted behaviour this change deliberately
reverses (ip:port must be rejected) or depended on a frontend variable
name (CSRF_TOKEN -> CSRF). Updated rather than left to fail, with the
reasoning recorded.

Known, not fixed: profile usernames don't propagate into discovery, so
peers display as a node id prefix rather than a name. Pre-existing,
separate from this work.

test_tui.py 72/72, test_security_fixes.py 29/29,
test_profile_creation.py 9/9, test_web_multi_profile.py 7/7,
test_ws_push.py 7/7.
